# If a ruby oidc-provider were leftovers in your freezer

This is not safe at any speed

## SAML 2.0 debug Identity Provider

The same Rack application serves OIDC and SAML. SAML is for isolated SSO
development, not production authentication. Configuration, private keys, and
pending logins live only in memory. Use one process/replica: restarting loses all
profiles and rotates their certificates. Reconfigure the SP after recreation.

Start with a nonempty password (also used by the existing OIDC login):

```sh
bundle install
export STATIC_PASSWORD="$(ruby -rsecurerandom -e 'print SecureRandom.hex(24)')"
export REDIRECT_URIS=http://localhost:4180/oauth2/callback
export SAML_DEBUG=true
bundle exec rackup config.ru -p 9292 -o 127.0.0.1
```

Configure one profile per full hostname/subdomain. Route those hostnames to this
deployment; for local testing, map `one.localhost` and `two.localhost` to
127.0.0.1 if your resolver does not already do so. Ports do not select profiles.
The current request hostname selects the profile; there is no cross-host listing
or mutation. Repeat these requests on another subdomain for another linkage.

```sh
curl -u "alice:$STATIC_PASSWORD" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"sp_entity_id":"urn:example:application","acs_url":"https://app.example.test/saml/acs"}' \
  http://one.localhost:9292/crud/saml

# View/export the profile, including all public IdP connection settings.
curl -u "alice:$STATIC_PASSWORD" \
  http://one.localhost:9292/crud/saml

# Update selected fields without rotating the signing certificate.
curl -u "alice:$STATIC_PASSWORD" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"name_id":"alice@example.test","attributes":{"email":"alice@example.test","groups":["users","admins"]}}' \
  http://one.localhost:9292/crud/saml

# Delete using POST (DELETE /crud/saml is also supported).
curl -u "alice:$STATIC_PASSWORD" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"action":"delete"}' \
  http://one.localhost:9292/crud/saml
```

POST accepts JSON or URL-encoded forms. Creation returns 201; partial updates
return 200; deletion returns 204. Supported configuration fields:

- `sp_entity_id`: required on creation; the application's exact SP entity ID.
- `acs_url`: the application's HTTP(S) Assertion Consumer Service URL.
  Defaults to the generated `<IdP origin>/saml/consume` for local inspection.
- `idp_base_url`: optional public origin, for example
  `https://one.idp.example.test`; its hostname must match the current profile.
  Defaults to the request origin, including its scheme and nonstandard port.
- `name_id`: emitted emailAddress NameID; defaults to `alice@example.com`.
- `attributes`: a map of names to strings or arrays of strings, replacing the
  default mock user's attributes. This changes asserted identity, not the login
  credentials: sign in with `alice` and `STATIC_PASSWORD`.

GET exports the Identity Provider SSO URL (`idp_sso_url`), Entity ID / Issuer URL
(`idp_entity_id`), and PEM X.509 certificate (`x509_certificate`). Copy these
into your application's SAML IdP configuration, or import `/saml/metadata`.
Exports never include private keys or passwords. They include generated,
read-only fields, so use only the supported fields above when posting updates.

Endpoints on each configured hostname:

| Endpoint | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| GET/POST `/crud/saml` | View/export, create, update, or POST-delete this host's profile |
| DELETE `/crud/saml` | Delete this host's profile |
| GET `/saml/metadata` | Public SAML 2.0 IdP metadata, SSO bindings, and signing certificate |
| GET `/saml/certificate` | Public PEM signing certificate |
| GET/POST `/saml/sso` | SP-initiated SSO with Redirect/POST AuthnRequests; GET without a request starts IdP-initiated SSO |
| GET/POST `/saml/consume` | Debug settings and decoded, **unverified** SAMLResponse/RelayState inspection |

`/crud/saml` and `/saml/consume` are available only when `SAML_DEBUG=true` and
require HTTP Basic authentication with `alice` / `STATIC_PASSWORD`. With debug
disabled they return 404. Do not expose this service to untrusted networks.
Use HTTPS outside local testing, and configure a trusted reverse proxy to strip
untrusted forwarded headers and preserve the intended hostname. SAML origins
are independent of `OIDC_HOST` / `OIDC_PORT` so subdomains remain distinct.

An ACS belongs to the **service provider**, not the IdP. Our GET consumer URL
shows debug configuration; GET or POST with a Base64 `SAMLResponse` inspects
XML. It never validates signatures, establishes a session, or treats submitted
claims as authenticated. Applications must use their own ACS to validate and
consume responses; outgoing responses always use HTTP-POST, not GET.

The login page exposes the decoded AuthnRequest. After login, inspect the signed
response and click **Continue to service provider** to submit it to the configured
ACS with the original RelayState. Both the Response and Assertion are signed
with RSA-SHA256 using that profile's ephemeral certificate. Assertions include
audience, recipient, request correlation, attributes, and a five-minute lifetime.
Login form tokens are single-use, expire after five minutes, and are invalidated
by profile changes. Certificates remain stable on updates; delete/recreate to
rotate them.

Limitations: this is a small SAML 2.0 browser-SSO subset, not a general federation
server. It accepts unsigned AuthnRequests; request signatures, when present,
are **not verified**. Requests are untrusted routing hints: issuer, destination,
and requested ACS must match the configured profile, and a password is always
required before issuance. There is no assertion encryption, Single Logout,
artifact binding, passive login, indexed ACS selection, or delegated scoping.
Only emailAddress NameIDs are emitted. Input XML is bounded to 64 KiB (including
after Redirect inflation), request bodies/queries to 128 KiB, and RelayState to
2 KiB. DTDs and external entity expansion are not allowed. There are at most 100
profiles and 1,000 pending login forms per process.

Requested authentication contexts must include the context this IdP actually
provides (`PasswordProtectedTransport` over HTTPS, otherwise `Password`), with
`exact` or `minimum` comparison. Stronger or unsupported contexts are rejected.

Run the regression suite (SAML signatures/flows, debug controls, and OIDC):

```sh
bundle exec ruby test/server_test.rb
```

## oauth2-proxy k8s container/service

```
apiVersion: v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: ...
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 5
      securityContext:
        runAsUser: 1000
        runAsGroup: 0
        fsGroup: 1
      containers:
      - name: "oauth2-proxy"
        env:
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_PROVIDER
          value: "oidc"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_ID
          value: "minimal-client"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_SECRET
          value: "..."
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_REDIRECT_URL
          value: "https://oauth2-proxy.localhost:4180/oauth2/callback"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_OIDC_ISSUER_URL # OAUTH2_PROXY_OIDC_ISSUER_URL
          value: "http://oidc-at-home.localhost:9292"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_SKIP_OIDC_DISCOVERY
          value: "true"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_LOGIN_URL
          value: "https://oidc-at-home.localhost:9292/authorize"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_REDEEM_URL
          value: "http://172.17.0.1:9292/token"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_OIDC_JWKS_URL
          value: "http://172.17.0.1:9292/jwks.json"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_PROFILE_URL
          value: "http://172.17.0.1:9292/userinfo"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_VALIDATE_URL
          value: "https://oidc-at-home.localhost:9292/validate"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET
          value: "..."
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_EMAIL_DOMAINS
          value: "*"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_UPSTREAMS
          value: "http://172.17.0.1:5601"
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_HTTP_ADDRESS
          value: "0.0.0.0:4180" # This matches this container
        - name: OAUTH2_PROXY_SKIP_PROVIDER_BUTTON
          value: "true"
        image: quay.io/oauth2-proxy/oauth2-proxy:latest
        args: ["--standard-logging=true", "--auth-logging=true", "--request-logging=true", "--set-xauthrequest=true"]
        imagePullPolicy: "IfNotPresent"
        ports:
        - name: oauth2-proxy
          containerPort: 4180
          protocol: TCP


kind: Service
metadata:
  name: ...
spec:
  ports:
  - name: oauth2-proxy
    port: 4180
    protocol: TCP
    targetPort: oauth2-proxy
  selector:
    app: ...
  type: LoadBalancer

```
